# Docker build-context excludes for backend/Dockerfile (PSY-1790).
#
# WHY IT LIVES HERE, NOT AT THE REPO ROOT. Docker reads `.dockerignore` from
# the root of the build CONTEXT, and both builds of this image use `backend/`
# as the context. A repo-root .dockerignore would be read by NEITHER, and
# would fail silently:
#   * CI      — .github/workflows/backend-image.yml sets `context: ./backend`.
#               Verifiable in-repo.
#   * Railway — the service's root directory is /backend, which is why
#               railway.toml's `dockerfilePath = "Dockerfile"` resolves. That
#               root directory is a DASHBOARD setting with no in-repo
#               representation, so nothing here can prove it. Confirm on the
#               next deploy: the build log's context-transfer line should read
#               ~26MB, not ~34MB. If it reads ~34MB this file is being
#               ignored, and the fix is where the file lives, not what's in it.
#
# Related trap: BuildKit prefers `<dockerfile>.dockerignore` over the
# context-root file when it exists. Creating `backend/Dockerfile.dockerignore`
# would silently disable every rule below. Nothing of that name exists today.
#
# DENY-LIST, DELIBERATELY. The builder stage runs `COPY . .` and then
# `go build ./cmd/server`, so the entire Go tree has to arrive. An allow-list
# would silently drop the next package someone adds. For the same reason,
# nothing here excludes `*_test.go` or `testdata/`: `go build` does not need
# them, but `go vet` and `go test` type-check them, so excluding them would
# plant a build-time-only break for whoever later adds a verification stage.
# Measured on this tree, that restraint costs 7.24MB of context and buys back
# ~0.3s, against a ~39s Go build (backend-image.yml).
#
# backend/Dockerfile is the source of truth for what the image needs; do not
# restate its COPY set here. Two constraints are NOT visible from it, and are
# the ones an edit to this file is most likely to break:
#
#   1. internal/services/geo/data/ — ~11MB of TSV reached only through
#      `//go:embed` in geo.go. No COPY names it; excluding it fails the
#      compile, not a COPY.
#   2. db/migrations/ is SQL, so never reach for a blanket `*.sql`.
#
# PATTERN ANCHORING, the other way to break this file. Docker matches with
# filepath.Match semantics, so `*` does NOT cross `/`: a bare `server` matches
# only <context>/server. That is load-bearing for the stray-binary block
# below — `**/server` or `**/main` would delete cmd/server/ and every other
# cmd/ package. Root-anchored names there, `**/` everywhere else, and the
# difference is deliberate in both directions.
#
# HOW WRONG EDITS SURFACE. An exclusion that breaks the compile fails the
# Backend Image Build job, which runs un-path-filtered on every PR — loud, and
# before Railway. An exclusion that only removes something a FUTURE stage would
# need (see the test-file note above) is invisible to that job, because the
# Dockerfile has no test or vet stage today. Those are the edits to think
# twice about.
#
# Re-measuring, since the numbers above are a point in time and are host- and
# arch-specific (measured on darwin/arm64):
#   docker buildx create --name ctx --driver docker-container
#   docker buildx build --builder ctx --progress=plain --target builder \
#     -f ./backend/Dockerfile ./backend 2>&1 | grep 'transferring context'

# Go coverage output. Written by scripts/coverage.sh and by CI's
# `go test -coverprofile=...`; never an input to anything. `coverage.sh`
# rewrites coverage.out on every run, so this also stops a local `COPY . .`
# cache bust per coverage run. If a future golden-file test ever adopts the
# conventional testdata/*.out extension, narrow this rule.
**/*.out
**/coverage.html

# Compiled host-arch binaries left in place by `go build ./cmd/...`, which
# drops one per package into backend/. A darwin/arm64 cmd/server measures
# 61MB — over twice the whole context — and busts `COPY . .` on every rebuild.
#
# This is a cache-and-size optimization, NOT a correctness guard, and it
# drifts: it is the full `ls backend/cmd` as of this commit, plus `main` (the
# Dockerfile's own -o target) and three legacy names whose packages are gone
# but whose binaries may still sit on an old machine. A new cmd/ package needs
# a line here and in the repo .gitignore. Nothing enforces that.
#
# ROOT-ANCHORED ON PURPOSE. See PATTERN ANCHORING above: adding `**/` to any
# line below would exclude the cmd/ source package of the same name.
main
apply-link-suggestions
backfill-artist-bandcamp-embeds
backfill-artist-discography
backfill-artist-location
backfill-artist-member-edges
backfill-artist-state
backfill-commons-photos
backfill-cover-art
backfill-entity-metro
backfill-release-links
backfill-slugs
backfill-spotify-images
backfill-venue-slugs
backfill-venue-timezones
community-gamma-sweep
dedup-radio-shows
dedup-shows
discovery-import
disparity-analysis
festival-entry
gen-api-token
gen-e2e-seed
geocode-venue-addresses
link-release-labels
openapi-spec
scaffold
scrape-import
seed
server
sweep-link-suggestions
upgrade-scene-scopes
verify-artist-states

# Local logs, database dumps, and backups. `**/` and not root-anchored,
# because `scripts/railway-migrate-prod.sh` writes `./backups/prod-backup-*.sql`
# relative to the caller's cwd with no `cd` guard, so a production pg_dump can
# land at any depth. `*-backup-*.sql` covers that script's filenames, which
# match none of the other patterns. Safe against db/migrations/: no migration
# name contains `_dump_` or `-backup-`, and there is no source directory
# called `backups`.
logs/
**/*.log
**/backups/
**/backup_*.tar.gz
**/*_dump_*.sql
**/*-backup-*.sql

# Credentials, at any depth. Root .gitignore already classifies these as
# secrets, so none can be tracked; this closes the same class for the LOCAL
# build, where untracked files do enter the context.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.crt
**/*.csr
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/.netrc
**/.npmrc
**/.git-credentials
**/id_rsa*

# Environment files. Nothing reads them at build time.
#
# Be precise about where this bites, because the obvious reading is wrong. CI
# and Railway build from a git checkout, and a real `.env` is gitignored, so
# only the four tracked `.env*.example`/`.template` files can exist there —
# on those legs this rule is inert. It matters for LOCAL `docker build`, where
# an untracked `.env` sits in the context. That is worth closing rather than
# reasoning about each time: the builder stage is discarded, but a discarded
# stage is still a cache layer, and on main the image job runs
# `cache-to: type=gha,mode=max`, which exports builder layers — context and
# all — into the shared GitHub Actions cache. The layer being thrown away is
# not the same as the bytes being gone.
**/.env*

# Local orchestration and host provisioning. Compose brings up dev/e2e/stage
# stacks from OUTSIDE the image, and systemd units target VMs, not containers.
docker-compose*.yml
systemd/

# Docs. Prose only; no build stage reads it. Excluding these is also what
# lets a docs-only PR hit the cached `COPY . .` instead of re-running the
# ~39s Go build in the always-run image job.
docs/
**/*.md

# Local databases and scratch copies. Mirrors the repo .gitignore's classes;
# db/migrations/ is unaffected (none of these extensions appear there).
**/*.db
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3
**/*.bak
**/*.backup
**/*.tmp
docker-volumes/
postgres-data/

# Editor / OS droppings. `**/` because Finder writes .DS_Store into whatever
# directory it browses, not just the root.
**/.DS_Store
**/*.swp
**/*.swo
.idea/
.vscode/

# A locally-installed dependency tree. The layout-deps stage runs its own
# `npm ci` from package.json + package-lock.json, so a host node_modules is
# both unused and (being built for the host arch) wrong.
**/node_modules/
